{
  "dom_id": "step-q314534",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14187947/assets/81175de5197dac2f2f037bde6985be619c68de75a574853c61de75316ae63971.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool is being drained. If its length and width are $32\\text{ m}$ and $12\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.4\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool? Assume that the drain rate is positive and do not round the answer.\n\nThe water is draining out at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=32\\,\\text{m},\\,W=12\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(32)(12)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =384\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-153.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $153.6\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$",
